From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maxim Stepanov <g.maxim.stepanoff@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 2 Jul 2026 12:35:52 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(db): sort by column ordinal and keep results when a
 re-query fails
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Header sorting was keyed by column NAME and injected as `order by "name"`,
which Postgres rejects as ambiguous for result sets with duplicate column
names (any join exposing two `id`s) — and the failed sort then wiped the
whole grid and stuck (`run()` only cleared the sort on SQL edits), so
re-running the unchanged SQL failed forever.

- Sort is now a ColumnSort keyed by 0-based column index, translated to a
  positional 1-based `ORDER BY n` in the paging wrapper; the name is kept
  only for the header chevron (which now also matches by index instead of
  lighting up every duplicate).
- dispatch_query carries a QueryIntent (Run/LoadMore/Sort). A failed sort
  or Load more keeps the loaded rows, shows the error above the still-
  populated grid, and reverts the requested sort to the one the rows
  actually reflect; only a fresh Run replaces the result on failure (and
  now also clears the sort so nothing wedges).
- Header clicks are ignored while a query is in flight: the grid still
  shows the previous result, so a click there must not wrap the in-flight
  SQL with an ordinal from the old column list (and cancel that run).
- The page size is snapshotted when a result session starts (page 0) and
  reused by Load more, so changing database.page_size mid-session cannot
  skew `page * page_size` offsets into duplicated or skipped rows.

